<h2>The dispute</h2>
<p>The parties married in 2017. The wife&rsquo;s application under Section 12 of the DV Act alleged abandonment and claimed she was without income. The Magistrate granted interim maintenance of Rs 12,500 per month under Section 23 read with Section 20. The husband appealed under Section 29 to the Court of Session at Dwarka, placing the wife&rsquo;s bank statements on record.</p>
<p>The Principal District and Sessions Judge found that the statements showed sustained balances exceeding Rs 6,00,000 across 2020&ndash;2023, with only minimal withdrawals — conduct inconsistent with the case of financial hardship pleaded before the Magistrate. Holding that a litigant &ldquo;cannot be allowed to take advantage of her own wrongs&rdquo;, the appellate court modified the order: spousal interim maintenance was declined, and Rs 6,000 per month was directed for the minor child alone.</p>

<h2>What the order illustrates</h2>
<div class="tiles">
<div class="tile"><strong>Disclosure is the foundation.</strong> Interim maintenance is decided on affidavits of income, assets and expenditure. A false or incomplete affidavit does not merely weaken the claim — it becomes the reason for its rejection.</div>
<div class="tile"><strong>The child is insulated.</strong> Whatever the findings against a parent&rsquo;s candour, the child&rsquo;s right to be maintained by both parents stands apart and was preserved in full.</div>
<div class="tile"><strong>Section 29 appeals are effective.</strong> The DV Act&rsquo;s internal appeal is a genuine merits remedy. Documents that were absent or ignored before the Magistrate can decisively alter the outcome before the Sessions Court.</div>
<div class="tile"><strong>Savings are not a shield for payers either.</strong> The same logic cuts both ways: respondents who understate income face adverse inferences and enhanced awards. The principle is symmetry of candour.</div>
</div>

<h2>The appellate route for DV maintenance orders</h2>
<div class="flow">
<div class="fstep"><strong>Order of the Magistrate.</strong> Interim or final relief under Sections 18&ndash;23 of the DV Act.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Appeal under Section 29.</strong> To the Court of Session within thirty days of service of the order — the forum that decided this case.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Further challenge.</strong> To the High Court in appropriate cases — typically confined to jurisdictional error or perversity, not a third round on facts.</div>
</div>

<div class="note"><p>Practice pointer for claimants: plead means honestly and explain balances — money held for a specific liability, borrowed funds, or family money parked in an account can all be explained, but only if disclosed. Practice pointer for respondents: obtain and analyse bank statements early; a well-documented Section 29 appeal filed within limitation is often more productive than a belated challenge in the High Court.</p></div>

<h2>Context for Delhi litigants</h2>
<p>Delhi&rsquo;s family and Mahila courts apply the affidavit-of-assets discipline rigorously, and appellate courts in the district judiciary — as this Dwarka order shows — are willing to recalibrate awards where the paper record contradicts the pleaded case. Maintenance remains a needs-and-means exercise: the aim is neither windfall nor penury, and the fastest way to lose the court&rsquo;s confidence on either side is an affidavit that the bank statements refute.</p>
<p>This article is for general information only and is not legal advice or a solicitation.</p>
